(233476, 13344, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Challenge:  Meets the challenge very well.  Really draws the eye up.  I might suggest tighter cropping at the top, as your eyes keep going to the sky instead of "resting" on the titans.<br/> <br/>Technique: Great angle, may want to consider getting down even lower for a dramatic effect.  The black and white works well for this as it emphasizes the age.  There is a little noise here, but nothing that detracts from the image.<br/> <br/>Overall: I think a tight top crop and fixing the noise would make this an EXCELLENT photo!</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB Challenge:  Meets the challenge very well.  Really draws the eye up.  I might suggest tighter cropping at the top, as your eyes keep going to the sky instead of "resting" on the titans. Technique: Great angle, may want to consider getting down even lower for a dramatic effect.  The black and white works well for this as it emphasizes the age.  There is a little noise here, but nothing that detracts from the image. Overall: I think a tight top crop and fixing the noise would make this an EXCELLENT photo!', '2003-03-10 14:20:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(233492, 13419, 6855, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB<br/> <br/>Challenge:  Meets the challenge, but the line is a little detracting, like it ends before it reaches the barn.  It''s a great landscape picture!<br/> <br/>Technique:  I can see you went to the end of the line which was good, but I think if you would  have crouched down and focused on the line instead of the barn, it would have been more impactful, while giving more attention to the line.  I would be curious how this picture looks in a sepia tone.   I think the colors, etc would make some great sepia hues.<br/> <br/>Overall:  A great job, and you followed the challenge rules well.  There is a little noise to clear up, but that''s easy to do "post-challenge".  I would be curious how this would look at a lower angle. <br/>''</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB Challenge:  Meets the challenge, but the line is a little detracting, like it ends before it reaches the barn.  It''s a great landscape picture! Technique:  I can see you went to the end of the line which was good, but I think if you would  have crouched down and focused on the line instead of the barn, it would have been more impactful, while giving more attention to the line.  I would be curious how this picture looks in a sepia tone.   I think the colors, etc would make some great sepia hues. Overall:  A great job, and you followed the challenge rules well.  There is a little noise to clear up, but that''s easy to do "post-challenge".  I would be curious how this would look at a lower angle. ''', '2003-03-10 14:24:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(233765, 13524, 6328, '<td valign="top">Greetings from Critique Club: Silver Fox<br/>I''m doing a critique on the flower, not on the Leading Lines Challenge as I do not believe that this fit the challenge.<br/> <br/>Personally, I love flower photos and the more natural, the better, such as the one you''ve captured here.  I think perhaps that you should have looked for one that you could have "shot" from above rather from below, as the whiteness of the sky has sort of blown the flower away from the person looking at it.<br/> <br/>You''ve captured the yellow nicely, but I would like to have seen some of the greenery around it at the same time.<br/> <br/>The focus and lighting are good, but I notice that you''ve cropped off a piece of the top petal, was this on purpose? or were you just going to large with it?<br/> <br/>What you have to remember sometimes is that BIGGER is not always best,sometimes keeping your photograph smaller and bringing in some of the background would enhance your flower with some visual impact, but only you can decide what is best.  There really isn''t an aspect of this flower photo that makes it stand out and shout "look at me", but at least you tried, and you entered it in the Challenge, so please keep on shooting and keep on trying your best to become better at what you''re doing. . .<br/>Sincerely,  Silver Fox</td>', 'Greetings from Critique Club: Silver FoxI''m doing a critique on the flower, not on the Leading Lines Challenge as I do not believe that this fit the challenge. Personally, I love flower photos and the more natural, the better, such as the one you''ve captured here.  I think perhaps that you should have looked for one that you could have "shot" from above rather from below, as the whiteness of the sky has sort of blown the flower away from the person looking at it. You''ve captured the yellow nicely, but I would like to have seen some of the greenery around it at the same time. The focus and lighting are good, but I notice that you''ve cropped off a piece of the top petal, was this on purpose? or were you just going to large with it? What you have to remember sometimes is that BIGGER is not always best,sometimes keeping your photograph smaller and bringing in some of the background would enhance your flower with some visual impact, but only you can decide what is best.  There really isn''t an aspect of this flower photo that makes it stand out and shout "look at me", but at least you tried, and you entered it in the Challenge, so please keep on shooting and keep on trying your best to become better at what you''re doing. . .Sincerely,  Silver Fox', '2003-03-10 15:57:22.000000', NULL, 0),
